@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1750 = private unnamed_addr constant [1394 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for class attributes and methods that use the same names as\0APython builtins.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AReusing a builtin name for the name of an attribute increases the\0Adifficulty of reading and maintaining the code, and can cause\0Anon-obvious errors, as readers may mistake the attribute for the\0Abuiltin and vice versa.\0A\0ASince methods and class attributes typically cannot be referenced directly\0Afrom outside the class scope, this rule only applies to those methods\0Aand attributes that both shadow a builtin _and_ are referenced from within\0Athe class scope, as in the following example, where the `list[int]` return\0Atype annotation resolves to the `list` method, rather than the builtin:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Class:\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def list() -> None:\0A        pass\0A\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def repeat(value: int, times: int) -> list[int]:\0A        return [value] * times\0A```\0A\0ABuiltins can be marked as exceptions to this rule via the\0A[`lint.flake8-builtins.ignorelist`] configuration option, or\0Aconverted to the appropriate dunder method. Methods decorated with\0A`@typing.override` or `@typing_extensions.override` are also\0Aignored.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Class:\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def list() -> None:\0A        pass\0A\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def repeat(value: int, times: int) -> list[int]:\0A        return [value] * times\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.flake8-builtins.ignorelist`\0A", align 1
