@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1877 = private unnamed_addr constant [776 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `for` loops with explicit loop-index variables that can be replaced\0Awith `enumerate()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen iterating over a sequence, it's often desirable to keep track of the\0Aindex of each element alongside the element itself. Prefer the `enumerate`\0Abuiltin over manually incrementing a counter variable within the loop, as\0A`enumerate` is more concise and idiomatic.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afruits = [\22apple\22, \22banana\22, \22cherry\22]\0Afor fruit in fruits:\0A    print(f\22{i + 1}. {fruit}\22)\0A    i += 1\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afruits = [\22apple\22, \22banana\22, \22cherry\22]\0Afor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\0A    print(f\22{i + 1}. {fruit}\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `enumerate`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#enumerate)\0A", align 1
