@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2069 = private unnamed_addr constant [1765 x i8] c"## What it does\0A\0AChecks for use of [PEP 695] type parameters with leading underscores in generic classes and\0Afunctions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A\0A[PEP 695] type parameters are already restricted in scope to the class or function in which they\0Aappear, so leading underscores just hurt readability without the usual privacy benefits.\0A\0AHowever, neither a diagnostic nor a fix will be emitted for \22sunder\22 (`_T_`) or \22dunder\22\0A(`__T__`) type parameter names as these are not considered private.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass GenericClass[_T]:\0A    var: _T\0A\0A\0Adef generic_function[_T](var: _T) -> list[_T]:\0A    return var[0]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass GenericClass[T]:\0A    var: T\0A\0A\0Adef generic_function[T](var: T) -> list[T]:\0A    return var[0]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix availability\0A\0AIf the name without an underscore would shadow a builtin or another variable, would be a\0Akeyword, or would otherwise be an invalid identifier, a fix will not be available. In these\0Asituations, you can consider using a trailing underscore or a different name entirely to satisfy\0Athe lint rule.\0A\0A## See also\0A\0AThis rule renames private [PEP 695] type parameters but doesn't convert pre-[PEP 695] generics\0Ato the new format. See [`non-pep695-generic-function`][UP047] and\0A[`non-pep695-generic-class`][UP046] for rules that will make this transformation.\0AThose rules do not remove unused type variables after their changes,\0Aso you may also want to consider enabling [`unused-private-type-var`][PYI018] to complete\0Athe transition to [PEP 695] generics.\0A\0A[PEP 695]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0695/\0A[UP047]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/non-pep695-generic-function\0A[UP046]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/non-pep695-generic-class\0A[PYI018]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/unused-private-type-var\0A", align 1
